    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by \"goblin's trick\" or \"goblin's surprise\"? That's a weird way to put it. Can you just explain the actual physiological reason why changing the range of motion helps with a plateau without the metaphors?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I was just trying to add a bit of flair to the explanation. The \"goblin's trick\" refers to the idea that changing the range of motion can help challenge the muscles in a new way, forcing them to adapt and grow.\n\nPhysiologically, when we're stuck in a plateau, it's often because our muscles have adapted to the specific movement pattern and weight we're using. By changing the range of motion, we're essentially changing the movement pattern, which can help to recruit different muscle fibers and challenge the muscles in a new way.\n\nFor example, if your client is stuck at a certain depth in the squat, increasing the depth can help to recruit the deeper hip and gluteal muscles, which may not be being fully utilized in the current movement pattern. This can help to break through the plateau and increase strength.\n\nIt's also worth noting that changing the range of motion can help to reduce the amount of muscle fiber recruitment required to lift the weight, which can make it feel easier and more manageable. This can be especially helpful for clients who are feeling fatigued or overwhelmed by the weight.\n\nBy the way, I was thinking about how some of the most effective squat variations, like the deficit squat or the sumo squat",
      "source": "target",
